  "reasoning_content": "We need answer user: \"Explain how a relational database executes a SQL query, from parsing and planning through indexing, joins, transactions, and returning rows. Write a detailed technical tutorial with concrete examples and tradeoffs, including common performance pitfalls. Use complete sentences and avoid repeating yourself.\"\n\nNeed produce detailed technical tutorial. Need final only. Need ensure complete sentences, avoid repeating. Need cover parsing, planning, indexing, joins, transactions, returning rows, examples tradeoffs pitfalls.\n\nWe can structure: overview, example database, query. Maybe use Postgres-ish. Include SQL: SELECT c.name, sum(o.amount) FROM customers c JOIN orders o ON c.id=o.customer_id WHERE o.region='us' AND o.created_at >= ... GROUP BY c.name HAVING sum(o.amount)>100000 ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 10;\n\nNeed explain pipeline: lexical analysis, parsing to AST, semantic analysis, planner/optimizer, execution engine, storage engine, indexes, access paths, joins algorithms, transaction isolation, locking/MVCC, commit, result retrieval, network. Tradeoffs and pitfalls.\n\nNeed concrete examples: indexes (B-tree, composite, partial, covering/index-only, bitmap? Hash? GiST/SPGIST), use EXPLAIN. Query examples and plans. Need avoid repeating. Could include sections:\n1. A running example\n2. Front end: parser, semantic analyzer\n3. Planner: logical plan, physical plan, cost model, statistics, join order, path selection, common mistakes (correlated subqueries, implicit row-by-row, select *, missing group by, cardinality misestimate, stale stats, volatile functions)\n4. Indexes: how used; equality/range/prefix; leftmost prefix; selectivity; index-only scan; covering index; partial index; expression index; tradeoffs write amplification, maintenance, bloat, duplicate indexes, overindexing; examples.\n5. Joins: nested loop, hash join, merge join; choice; memory; work memory; skew; join order; outer joins; CTEs/materialization; subqueries; anti-semi-join; pitfalls.\n6. Transactions: isolation levels, MVCC, snapshots, locks, concurrency, deadlocks, read committed vs repeatable read/serializable, write skew, phantom reads; locking; commit; WAL; recovery. How query sees rows; consistent snapshots; uncommitted rows invisible. Tradeoffs consistency/perf.\n7. Returning rows",
